{"id":4854,"date":"2026-06-04T11:44:11","date_gmt":"2026-06-04T10:44:11","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/?p=4854"},"modified":"2026-06-04T11:44:12","modified_gmt":"2026-06-04T10:44:12","slug":"zaha-hadid-architects-at-william-tyndale-primary-school-retrofitting-the-new-quentin-blake-centre-for-illustration","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/zaha-hadid-architects-at-william-tyndale-primary-school-retrofitting-the-new-quentin-blake-centre-for-illustration\/","title":{"rendered":"Zaha Hadid Architects at William Tyndale Primary\u00a0School: Retrofitting the New Quentin Blake Centre for Illustration\u00a0"},"content":{"rendered":"\n<div class=\"wp-block-stackable-image stk-block-image stk-block stk-2655fe8\" data-block-id=\"2655fe8\"><figure><span class=\"stk-img-wrapper stk-image--shape-stretch\"><img decoding=\"async\" class=\"stk-img wp-image-4861 lazyload\" data-src=\"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/1-1.png\" width=\"2220\" height=\"1248\" data-srcset=\"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/1-1.png 2220w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/1-1-300x169.png 300w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/1-1-1024x576.png 1024w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/1-1-768x432.png 768w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/1-1-1536x863.png 1536w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/1-1-2048x1151.png 2048w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/1-1-356x200.png 356w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/1-1-200x112.png 200w\" data-sizes=\"(max-width: 2220px) 100vw, 2220px\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml;base64,PHN2ZyB3aWR0aD0iMSIgaGVpZ2h0PSIxIiB4bWxucz0iaHR0cDovL3d3dy53My5vcmcvMjAwMC9zdmciPjwvc3ZnPg==\" style=\"--smush-placeholder-width: 2220px; --smush-placeholder-aspect-ratio: 2220\/1248;\" \/><\/span><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Zaha Hadid Architects (ZHA) recently embarked on their third co-design with Year 5 children at William Tyndale Primary School in Islington. Architects Angela Augelli<strong> <\/strong>Curci and Effie Nakajima led the project, setting a timely brief: Retrofit an Art Gallery for Floods and Heatwaves, based on the soon to be open Quentin Blake Centre for Illustration.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<blockquote class=\"wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow\">\n<p class=\"has-light-grey-background-color has-background has-small-font-size wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>&#8220;What surprised me most was how easily the children started to connect design with the environment&#8230; By the final project, they were naturally thinking about sustainability as part of their designs, without being reminded.&#8221;<\/strong> &#8211; <em>Ange<\/em>la Augelli Curci<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">At the outset, Effie and Angela introduced the core principles of sustainable architecture and how designers can mitigate risks in the face of a changing climate. The children explored the essential elements of &#8220;green&#8221; building &#8211; from materials\u2019 lifecycles and water usage to renewable energy and waste management. This provided a unique opportunity for the students to see their earlier climate science lessons applied to a real-world context, whilst gaining an understanding of scale, elevation, and landscaping.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The Workshops<\/h4>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><\/p>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"576\" data-src=\"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/4-1024x576.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-4864 lazyload\" data-srcset=\"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/4-1024x576.png 1024w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/4-300x169.png 300w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/4-768x432.png 768w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/4-1536x863.png 1536w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/4-2048x1151.png 2048w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/4-356x200.png 356w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/4-200x112.png 200w\" data-sizes=\"(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml;base64,PHN2ZyB3aWR0aD0iMSIgaGVpZ2h0PSIxIiB4bWxucz0iaHR0cDovL3d3dy53My5vcmcvMjAwMC9zdmciPjwvc3ZnPg==\" style=\"--smush-placeholder-width: 1024px; --smush-placeholder-aspect-ratio: 1024\/576;\" \/><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><em>Children visit the Serpentine North with ZHA architects for field research<\/em><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A key focus of the project was the concept of &#8220;retrofit&#8221; &#8211; the process of re-purposing, adapting and\/or extending an existing building. The pupils learned that while building from scratch with recycled materials is an option, adapting existing structures is often the most sustainable choice, as it significantly reduces waste and energy consumption.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">To make the project tangible, it was vital for the children to see a successful retrofit. Although nearby, the Quentin Blake Centre for Illustration was a construction site, so ZHA took sixty Year 5s to the Serpentine North Gallery in Kensington \u2013 one of ZHA\u2019s dramatic retrofits. The trip allowed the children to see firsthand how contemporary design can coexist with an historic building.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Equipped with&nbsp;basic&nbsp;climate science,&nbsp;nascent&nbsp;architectural knowledge and&nbsp;an&nbsp;inspirational&nbsp;research&nbsp;trip, the 9-and-10-year-olds set to work as&nbsp;designers.&nbsp;Forming small teams, they began translating their ideas into detailed drawings.&nbsp;<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Inspired by the Centre\u2019s namesake,&nbsp;teams&nbsp;incorporated whimsical yet functional features, such as&nbsp;Matilda-themed library corners&nbsp;and a&nbsp;&#8220;James and the Giant Peach&#8221; dome.&nbsp;Managing to remain&nbsp;focused on the climate brief,&nbsp;many designs featured solar panels for renewable energy, accessible lifts for inclusivity, and&nbsp;an extension&nbsp;raised on stilts to&nbsp;avoid&nbsp;flooding.&nbsp;<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The project culminated in formal presentations&nbsp;by&nbsp;the child-teams&nbsp;to&nbsp;explain&nbsp;to a panel of experts&nbsp;their design choices. They&nbsp;spoke&nbsp;with remarkable confidence&nbsp;in response to&nbsp;the experts\u2019&nbsp;questions about sustainability and spatial planning.&nbsp;<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h4 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The Reveal<\/h4>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-stackable-image stk-block-image stk-block stk-e096ce3\" data-block-id=\"e096ce3\"><figure><span class=\"stk-img-wrapper stk-image--shape-stretch\"><img decoding=\"async\" class=\"stk-img wp-image-4869 lazyload\" data-src=\"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/1-2.png\" width=\"1366\" height=\"768\" data-srcset=\"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/1-2.png 1366w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/1-2-300x169.png 300w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/1-2-1024x576.png 1024w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/1-2-768x432.png 768w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/1-2-356x200.png 356w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/1-2-200x112.png 200w\" data-sizes=\"(max-width: 1366px) 100vw, 1366px\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml;base64,PHN2ZyB3aWR0aD0iMSIgaGVpZ2h0PSIxIiB4bWxucz0iaHR0cDovL3d3dy53My5vcmcvMjAwMC9zdmciPjwvc3ZnPg==\" style=\"--smush-placeholder-width: 1366px; --smush-placeholder-aspect-ratio: 1366\/768;\" \/><\/span><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><em>The overall winner&#8217;s design rendered by ZHA architects<\/em> <em>with children&#8217;s design elements overlayed<\/em><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Following the&nbsp;children\u2019s&nbsp;presentations, Effie,&nbsp;Angela&nbsp;and ZHA colleagues interpreted the children&#8217;s concepts, using AI software to aid the visualisation process. Angela and Effie returned to school to show the children&nbsp;and their families the results.&nbsp;Year 5 was&nbsp;utterly amazed&nbsp;and full of excitement&nbsp;as they recognised their ideas in polished, professional form.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The celebration concluded with an awards ceremony, recognising excellence in categories such as:&nbsp;<\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Best Use of Materials&nbsp;&nbsp;<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Best Use of Space&nbsp;<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Most Imaginative Idea&nbsp;<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Future Architect Award&nbsp;<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Best Creative Presentation.&nbsp;<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large is-resized\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"576\" data-src=\"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/3-1024x576.png\" alt=\"\" class=\"wp-image-4863 lazyload\" style=\"--smush-placeholder-width: 1024px; --smush-placeholder-aspect-ratio: 1024\/576;width:1180px;height:auto\" data-srcset=\"https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/3-1024x576.png 1024w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/3-300x169.png 300w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/3-768x432.png 768w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/3-1536x863.png 1536w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/3-2048x1151.png 2048w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/3-356x200.png 356w, https:\/\/cc-ac.org\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/06\/3-200x112.png 200w\" data-sizes=\"(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml;base64,PHN2ZyB3aWR0aD0iMSIgaGVpZ2h0PSIxIiB4bWxucz0iaHR0cDovL3d3dy53My5vcmcvMjAwMC9zdmciPjwvc3ZnPg==\" \/><\/figure>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><em><em>Yr 5 child explains their design concept at the Reveal display<\/em><\/em><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Parents,&nbsp;guardians&nbsp;and teachers were invited to view a&nbsp;display&nbsp;of the children\u2019s original drawings&nbsp;pinned up&nbsp;alongside the ZHA professional&nbsp;renders.&nbsp;&nbsp;<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">We are deeply grateful to ZHA for&nbsp;this&nbsp;successful collaboration&nbsp;with&nbsp;William Tyndale Primary School, and&nbsp;thank&nbsp;everyone&nbsp;involved for&nbsp;facilitating&nbsp;an inspiring and positive co-design experience.&nbsp;<\/p>\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Zaha Hadid Architects (ZHA) embark on their third co-design with Year 5 children at William Tyndale Primary School in Islington. Architects Angela Augelli Curci and Effie Nakajima led the project, setting a timely brief: Retrofit an Art Gallery for Floods and Heatwaves, based on the soon to be open Quentin Blake Centre for Illustration.<\/p>\n","protected":false},"author":10,"featured_media":4861,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"open","sticky":false,"template":"","format":"standard","meta":{"_acf_changed":false,"footnotes":""},"categories":[12],"tags":[51,44,54,19,55,45,50,43,53,52,20],"class_list":["post-4854","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","category-ccac-news","tag-climate-change-charity","tag-climate-education","tag-climate-initiative","tag-co-design","tag-north-london-climate","tag-primary-education","tag-primary-school","tag-sustainable-architecture","tag-william-tyndale","tag-zaha-hadid-architects-pro-bono","tag-zha"],"acf":[],"yoast_head":"<!-- This site is optimized with the Yoast SEO plugin v28.5 - https:\/\/yoast.com\/product\/yoast-seo-wordpress\/ -->\n<title>Zaha 